WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.080
There are lots of good reasons
to try and follow a healthier diet—

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.160
you lose weight,
you feel good,

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.960
but the main reason?

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.520
To live a longer, happier,
more productive life.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.560
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.760
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.000
Can’t live with them,
can’t live without them.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.280
And yes, I’m talking
about teeth.

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:29.720
Did you know that chewing pressures
in the jaw can have neurological effects?

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.840
Here’s our first story.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.280
Systematic reviews
and meta-analyses

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.280
have found that tooth loss,
or periodontitis,

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.480
is associated with
both cognitive impairment

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.880
and dementia.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.240
Reverse causation might be
an intuitive explanation,

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.480
dementia leading to a decline
in oral hygiene,

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.600
but prospective studies
following people over time

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:53.680
have found that tooth loss

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.040
appears to predict
future cognitive decline,

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:00.400
and the more missing teeth,
the higher the associated risk.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.600
Genetic determinants
of elevated periodontitis risk

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.120
do not appear
to be associated

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.320
with the development
of Alzheimer's disease,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:09.120
suggesting the association
may be due

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.920
to shared
or confounding risk factors.

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:15.240
A poor diet
or low socioeconomic status

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.560
represent common pathways for
both tooth loss and dementia,

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.160
but even studies controlling
for confounding factors

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.320
found a connection
between tooth loss and dementia.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.080
However, one factor that wasn't
controlled for was intelligence.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:33.320
Those with higher IQ
are both more likely to floss,

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.280
and have fewer
tooth and gum problems

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.280
as well as have
a delayed onset

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.080
of clinically
detectable Alzheimer's

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.680
due to
a greater cognitive reserve.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.680
Are there any
potential causal mechanisms

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.520
beyond the chronic inflammation?

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.280
If you induce periodontitis
in mice with P. gingivalis,

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.360
the keystone pathogen
in human periodontitis,

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:01.240
the bacteria
ends up in their brains

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.400
and triggers an increase

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.640
in the Alzheimer's-associated
plaque protein amyloid beta.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.760
Since then,
evidence of the bacteria

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.040
has been found
in human Alzheimer's brains.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.480
Clinical trials
are now underway

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.680
to see if blocking
its neurotoxic effects

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:18.480
will have
any therapeutic benefit.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.440
A clue to
a stranger potential mechanism

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.240
was unearthed in
a subgroup analysis

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.320
of a tooth loss
and dementia meta-analysis.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.680
Only tooth loss
among those without dentures

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:33.760
was associated with the
later development of dementia.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.600
The same tooth loss
with dentures was not.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.560
How does that make any sense?

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.960
A review entitled
"Mastication for the Mind"

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.280
compiled a number of studies

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.000
suggesting that chewing,
mastication in medical speak,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.400
is somehow important
for proper brain function.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.040
For example, the extraction
of teeth from aged mice

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.080
appears to upset
their memory and learning.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.120
But maybe they're just upset,
in pain, inflamed,

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:00.920
from having their teeth removed.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.080
A more convincing series
of experiments

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.040
compared mice
given food pellets

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:07.800
versus the same pellets
crushed into powder.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.800
Those eating the "soft" diet,
the powdered diet,

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:16.360
also suffered a loss in memory
and learning capacity.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:20.160
What does chewing
have to do with cognition?

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.320
Stick an ultrasound probe
on someone's head

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:25.400
and you can detect an increase
in cerebral blood flow

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.720
of the largest artery branch
in the brain

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.920
when they clench their jaw
or chew gum.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:32.560
In an fMRI machine,

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:35.400
brain scans show
enhanced cognitive arousal

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.400
when chewing even tasteless,
sugarless gum,

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.280
though translational studies
on the acute cognitive effects

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.160
of gum chewing
are contradictory.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.505
Maybe it's not the chewing,
though.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:48.600
Noticing that people
missing molars

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.200
on one side of their mouths

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.120
have
asymmetrically-sized pupils,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:56.720
a smaller pupil
on the missing molar side,

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.080
researchers tested whether
implanting dental crowns

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.760
to replace the missing teeth

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.880
could reduce the discrepancy,
and it did.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.360
The study, subtitled "New Teeth
for a Brighter Brain,"

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.880
suggested that a gap
in the sensation of teeth

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.880
pushing against one another

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.600
adversely affects
brain function.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.400
To put it to the test,

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.640
we'd want to randomize
people with missing teeth

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:22.440
to get dentures

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.640
and see if their cognition
improves over those who don't.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.560
No such studies exist,

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.920
but there was an intriguing
pilot study performed.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.080
Ten toothless individuals,

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.720
9 out of 10 of them
cognitively impaired,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.800
6 of them severely so,

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:41.360
were given conventional dentures
for a month

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:43.400
before being fitted
with overdentures,

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.840
which are snapped
into titanium implants

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.480
surgically screwed
into the jawbone.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:49.880
The conventional dentures,

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:53.040
held in place by adhesives
and natural suction,

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.360
did nothing to significantly
alter cognitive function.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.840
But the ones securely attached
to implants in the bone,

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:01.240
presumably transmitting
the same kind

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:03.880
of chewing pressure sensations
to the nerves in the jaw

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:07.040
that natural roots
of teeth might,

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.760
had a dramatic effect.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.440
Nine out of ten
of the subjects

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:12.240
went into the study
cognitively impaired,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.440
but 8 out of 10 left the study
cognitively intact.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:20.240
This suggests well-fitting,
secure dental prosthesis

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.480
isn't just about improving
self-confidence,

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:24.360
social contact,
quality of life,

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:28.080
but proper brain functioning
as well.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:29.160
Of course, even better, though,

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:33.240
would be to preserve
the teeth you have.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:34.040
In our next story,

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:37.920
we look at what would happen
if you stopped brushing your teeth

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.400
but ate healthier.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.720
Experimentally, if you have people
stop brushing their teeth,

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:45.800
plaque starts to
build up and within

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:48.680
a few days the gums
start to get inflamed.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:50.400
You can’t see anything yet,

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.600
but if you take a
biopsy at the gum line

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.240
you can see the inflammation
starting to spread,

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:57.640
and within a few weeks
overt gingivitis

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:59.280
becomes apparent,
where your gums can get

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.600
red, swollen, and bleed easily.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:03.320
And if you don’t do
anything about it,

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:05.480
you can develop periodontal disease,

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:08.000
where the inflammation
creeps down into

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.720
the supporting structures of the tooth—

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:13.600
the bone and ligaments--
setting you up for tooth loss.

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:15.400
OK, but how did we get along

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:19.120
for millions of years
without brushing?

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:22.280
Yeah, dental disease is
almost universal these days,

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:24.400
but thousands of years
before the invention

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.480
of the toothbrush there are
skulls with perfect teeth.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:30.520
Now, you can say:
“yeah, but that was also

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:33.680
thousands of years before the
invention of candy bars,”

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:35.840
but you don’t know until...

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:39.440
you put it to the test.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:41.360
OK, yeah, but where
are you going to find

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:43.280
people to not only stop brushing

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:47.440
but also to forgo eating processed junk?

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.200
Security guards.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.920
That‘s how you do it.

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:53.600
It was one of these
survivor-type TV shows

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:56.600
where people were forced to
live under stone-age conditions;

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:58.840
so no toothbrushes,
toothpaste, dental floss,

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:02.440
toothpicks, or other oral
hygiene products for a month.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:04.480
Now they could use
a twig or something

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:06.400
but they were pretty
much on their own.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:08.520
But, no candy bars either.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.240
They were going for about 4,000 BC;

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:13.840
so lots of whole grains,
with supplemental salt,

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:15.680
herbs, honey, milk, and meat,

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:17.560
and then they could go
out and pick berries

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:19.080
or see what they could catch.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.640
So, what happened?

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:26.320
With no oral hygiene
their plaque built up,

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:29.920
but their gums got healthier.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:33.520
This is measuring BOP,
bleeding on probing

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:36.640
(whether or not your gums bled
when poked with a dental tool),

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:38.440
a measure of gingivitis;

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:41.523
and in almost every
case they got better.

00:07:41.634 --> 00:07:44.080
Many of the more
disease-causing bacteria

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:47.120
seemed to have disappeared
from their mouths.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:50.560
They suggest this could be
from the lack of refined sugars,

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:51.960
but they were eating honey;

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:54.840
so it wasn’t a
a sugar-free diet.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.200
Ah, but what they
were eating was lots

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:00.640
of whole grains and berries
rich in antioxidant phytonutrients

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:02.840
with anti-inflammatory properties.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:04.200
So, maybe it was a combination:

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:06.480
sugar intake restriction combined with

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.840
the intake of really healthy foods.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:11.300
Thus, all those experimental
studies where people stop brushing

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:13.760
and their gums inevitably get inflamed

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:16.440
may only be applicable
for people eating

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:18.480
lots of processed foods rich in sugar

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:22.520
and low in anti-inflammatory
whole plant foods.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:25.520
What about the role of
nutrition in periodontal health?

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:28.200
Gingivitis can lead to periodontitis,

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.440
an inflammatory disease
of the supporting tissues

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:32.680
of the teeth, which if untreated,

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:34.920
can lead to the progressive
loss of the bone

00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:36.920
that holds the teeth in place.

00:08:36.920 --> 00:08:39.040
Now, part of the development
of periodontal disease

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:41.360
may involve oxidative stress;

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:43.240
so, not only do we need
to reduce our intake

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:47.080
of pro-inflammatory foods like
refined carbs and saturated fats,

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:49.640
maybe it would help
if we sought out foods

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:52.280
that are antioxidant rich.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:56.600
So, is there an association
between dietary vitamin C intake,

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.440
for example, with periodontitis?

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:01.520
Apparently so, with
increasing risk of periodontitis

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:04.520
associated with lower
levels of vitamin C intake,

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:06.040
but you don’t know until...

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:08.800
you put it to the
test to figure out

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:11.560
what effect vitamin C
depletion and supplementation

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:14.080
would have on periodontal health.

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:16.160
They basically locked
everyone up for three months

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:17.680
so they can provide
controlled amounts

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.840
of vitamin C and measures
of gum inflammation

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:24.960
were directly related
to their vitamin C status.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.640
On about one orange
worth of vitamin C

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:29.400
their gums improved,

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.600
but then down to 5 mg
a day they got worse,

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:36.400
but then on 10 oranges worth
a day they got better,

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:39.880
and then worse again
when back down to 5.

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:45.200
Pretty convincing, though 5 mg
a day is like scurvy level.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.520
I mean we know that
your gums start bleeding,

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:49.760
your teeth could fall out
when you have scurvy

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:53.080
but that doesn’t mean
taking extra helps.

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:56.040
And indeed, 1,500 mg
of vitamin C a day

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.840
did not seem to help prevent gingivitis.

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:03.840
And even 2,000 a day failed to
help periodontitis sufferers.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.640
Maybe vitamin C is just too
weak of an antioxidant?

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:10.440
OK, what about lycopene, the
powerful antioxidant pigment

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:13.040
that makes tomatoes red?

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:14.800
It worked!

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.440
But that was from
injecting the stuff directly

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:20.360
into the gum pocket with a syringe.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:23.760
Does it work if you just eat it?

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.240
Let’s find out.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:28.520
A randomized, placebo-
controlled clinical trial

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:32.200
on the efficacy of lycopene
in the treatment of gingivitis.

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:36.080
Two weeks of less than a
single tomato-a-day’s worth

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:39.360
of lycopene versus
placebo for two weeks

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:42.000
both along with the
standard dental treatment,

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:44.720
which helped—a 10 to 20%
reduction in gingivitis

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:47.800
in the placebo group,
but nearly 30% improvement

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:50.800
within just one week
in the lycopene group.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:53.120
And this was just like
the amount of lycopene

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:56.800
found in a teaspoon and a
half of tomato paste a day.

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:58.400
Totally doable.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:00.760
OK, so tomatoes may help with gingivitis.

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:03.680
What about periodontitis?

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:06.240
Another randomized, double-blind,
placebo-controlled trial

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:08.800
again treated with
usual dental cleaning

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:11.400
plus either that one
tomato-a-day’s worth

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:15.280
of lycopene or a placebo
for two months and…

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:18.520
significant improvements in
plaque gingivitis and bleeding,

00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:22.058
though not probe pocket
depth and clinical attachment.

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:25.120
They conclude that
supplementation with lycopene

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:27.240
seems to have augmented
the healing sequence

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:29.240
of inflamed gingival tissues.

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:33.760
OK, but that was with a
whole tomato’s worth a day.

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:35.640
How about half-a-tomato’s worth,

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:37.760
or just three-quarters of a teaspoon

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:40.920
of tomato paste worth of lycopene a day?

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:44.080
it didn’t work, no difference,

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:48.600
so looks like you have
to go the whole tomato.

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:49.360
Finally today,

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:55.080
plant-based diets are put to the test
in the treatment of periodontal disease.

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:58.800
What's the effect of nutrition
on periodontal disease?

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:01.960
Periodontal disease is a
bacterial infection that results

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:05.920
in inflammatory destruction of
the connective tissue and bone

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:07.120
that supports the teeth,

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:10.680
and is therefore one of the leading
causes of our teeth falling out.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:15.600
Like most infections though, how our
body responds may play a critical role.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:18.280
Yes, the presence of bacteria
is the primary cause,

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:23.400
but a susceptible host is also
necessary for disease initiation.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:27.240
The standard explanation of periodontal
disease is the plaque theory:

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:31.320
the buildup of plaque leads to
gingivitis, gum inflammation,

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:34.080
which leads to periodontitis,
inflammation lower down

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:36.360
beneath the gums.
But in some forms

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:40.600
of periodontal disease plaque
doesn't appear to play a critical role.

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:43.600
Therefore, in the last few years,
there's been more interest

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:47.480
in the importance of systemic
health, our body's response.

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:51.360
In this respect, nutrition may
be of great importance

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:54.080
since it's been implicated in a number
of other inflammatory diseases,

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:57.520
all of which carry elevated
periodontal disease risk.

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:00.960
Traditionally, when we think of the
effects of nutrition on dental diseases,

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:03.600
we're only thinking about cavities.

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:08.160
However, there's been less research on
the role of diet in periodontal diseases.

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:13.120
Well, if it's about inflammation, one
would expect saturated fat-rich diets

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:15.400
to make things worse,
increasing oxidative stress

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:18.160
as well inflammation,
so we may want to cut down

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:20.760
on saturated fat.
But, look, let's not just speculate.

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:25.160
I mean is there an association between
cholesterol levels and periodontitis?

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:27.800
If not, it would be hard
to implicate saturated fat.

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:30.280
But no, there does
appear to be a link.

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:34.880
Those with high cholesterol do
appear to have up to double the risk.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:37.660
What about periodontal
conditions in vegetarians?

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:41.320
A 100 vegetarians versus
non-vegetarians were studied,

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:44.520
and those eating vegetarian did
have better periodontal conditions

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:47.320
(less inflammation signs,
less periodontal damage,

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:49.680
and better dental home care).

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:51.720
However, it should be
considered that vegetarians

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:53.360
may not just be avoiding meat,

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:57.920
but are healthier in other ways,
like better dental home care.

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:02.240
But do people who eat more
saturated fat get more periodontitis?

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:05.200
Yes, about double the risk at
the highest levels of intake,

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:08.000
and this study was in Japan
where they eat less than half

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:10.160
the meat and dairy
compared to the US.

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:13.840
The only way to know for sure, though,
is to do an interventional trial

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:16.080
where you change people's
diets and see what happens.

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:19.200
In other words, you
have to put it to the test.

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:22.080
And bone loss was indeed
magnified by a diet

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:23.866
high in saturated fat
and cholesterol.

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:27.920
"A high-fiber, low-fat diet improves
periodontal disease markers"

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:30.600
in terms of probing depth,
clinical attachment loss,

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:33.600
and bleeding on probing—
all the standard measures.

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:36.800
And, of course, eating a healthier diet,
body weight, blood sugar control,

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:40.640
and systemic inflammation
improved as well.

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:42.600
Ah, but that complicates things.

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:45.800
Maybe their mouths got better just
because they lost so much weight?

00:14:45.800 --> 00:14:47.360
You can improve
periodontal disease

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:51.120
with just bariatric surgery,
like stomach stapling.

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:54.520
Well, after eight weeks on the diet,
they went back on their regular diet

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:56.400
and so gained most
of that weight back,

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:59.840
but the periodontal disease
improvements persisted, suggesting

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:03.880
that it was more than just the weight
loss that lead to the improvements.

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:07.200
They're thinking maybe the high-fiber
diet altered their good gut flora,

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:10.880
or maybe their oral flora?
What exactly was going on?

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:14.000
Well, German researchers
took 20 women with mild

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:15.600
to moderate chronic periodontitis,

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:17.840
and for a year tried
to transition their diets

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:20.640
towards more wholesome nutrition,
meaning more plant foods,

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:23.720
more whole foods, more fresh foods,
trying to center their diets around

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:25.840
vegetables and fruit,
whole grains, potatoes,

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:28.040
and legumes—beans, split
peas, chickpeas and lentils.

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:31.800
And after 12 months, the patients
showed a significant reduction

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:34.480
of probing pocket depth,
gingival inflammation,

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:37.400
and measured for the first time
decreased concentrations

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:42.000
of inflammatory chemicals inside the
crevice between the tooth and gums,

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:44.320
which are thought responsible
for the tissue destruction

00:15:44.320 --> 00:15:48.400
in periodontal disease, a
decrease by as much as 75%.

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:52.360
And all the while, their oral
hygiene status didn't change,

00:15:52.360 --> 00:15:55.598
suggesting it was
the diet that did it.

